02:48.080 --> 02:51.080
And more importantly, let me fix this part first,

02:51.120 --> 02:52.440
you'll notice that because we're not actually

02:52.440 --> 02:53.600
calling props anywhere,

02:53.600 --> 02:54.960
there's no actually need to do that,

02:54.960 --> 02:57.760
so you can actually just leave it off like this right now.

02:57.760 --> 03:02.760
So, but the question here around define props,

03:02.880 --> 03:04.840
why is it not yelling at us

03:04.840 --> 03:07.440
that we don't know what this is looking for?

03:07.440 --> 03:08.960
So the reason this is the case

03:08.960 --> 03:10.800
is because when you're inside of script setup,

03:10.800 --> 03:12.960
basically the compiler knows

03:12.960 --> 03:15.280
that these specific functions are gonna exist.

03:15.280 --> 03:17.360
So in other words, define props and define emits,

03:17.360 --> 03:19.000
which I actually didn't clarify earlier,

03:19.000 --> 03:21.320
is only specific to script setup.

03:21.320 --> 03:22.560
If you try using define emits

03:22.560 --> 03:24.160
to find props inside of an options API,

03:24.160 --> 03:26.600
that'll just break, because why would you?

03:26.600 --> 03:29.560
If you're in options API leveraging the setup,

03:29.560 --> 03:31.800
then you should already be using the other options.

03:31.800 --> 03:32.880
And so in other words,

03:32.880 --> 03:35.160
the compiler knows that it's technically already a global,

03:35.160 --> 03:37.840
so treat it like a global import for this.

03:37.840 --> 03:40.920
That said though, I personally am typically a fan,

03:40.920 --> 03:42.800
especially if this is a newer code base,

03:42.800 --> 03:45.320
to just leave the import explicit.

03:45.320 --> 03:47.400
That way no one knew is like, wait, what's going on?

03:47.400 --> 03:50.320
There's no assumed knowledge here, we're good.

03:50.320 --> 03:52.560
So why is reactive not like that?

03:52.560 --> 03:53.960
Why is reactive not like that?

03:53.960 --> 03:57.000
So we delete that and save.

03:57.000 --> 04:00.560
The reason for that is because reactive cannot be assumed

04:00.560 --> 04:04.240
in this context that it will be imported.

04:04.240 --> 04:05.960
So in other words, to think of it another way

04:05.960 --> 04:09.000
is that when this code is built and compiled,

04:09.000 --> 04:11.080
define props basically just reverts back

04:11.080 --> 04:11.920
to its normal stuff.

04:11.920 --> 04:14.000
Like there's, if it's not detected,

04:14.000 --> 04:16.200
it'll get taken out basically.

04:16.200 --> 04:17.680
So there's some kind of optimization happening.

04:17.680 --> 04:19.360
I can't explain the details of it,

04:19.360 --> 04:21.200
but there's a reason why the fine props is okay,

04:21.200 --> 04:23.840
but then other helper methods like ref, reactive,

04:23.840 --> 04:26.560
view cannot intelligently guess those things.

04:26.560 --> 04:28.880
So as a result, it forces you to be very explicit

04:28.880 --> 04:30.880
that you want to use these helper methods.

04:32.760 --> 04:33.600
So yeah, they're called, I think,

04:33.600 --> 04:37.320
compiler-specific helper functions, but anyways.

04:37.320 --> 04:38.640
And then the other thing we go on in practice

04:38.640 --> 04:41.320
is define emits, and that we can just say,

04:41.320 --> 04:45.080
once again, we can say something like update user list.

